WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.200
And when we got that permit, we built the tower on top of the Life Sciences building.

2
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.420
And it was built by Paul Burt—who was a radio engineer,

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:27.300
not a physical engineer—two other students who had some engineering background with actual mechanical engineers . . . Paul Burt,

4
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.300
Tony Larosa, Miles Jackson, Elizabeth Chinn Dade . . . Who else was there?

5
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.660
Of course myself and David [Crawford], and there were a couple of other people who helped alternately.

6
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.340
We put it up in about the course of a week.

7
00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.320
We’d go up there after the Life Science building closed.

8
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.080
We’d load all the gear onto the freight elevator.

9
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:48.860
We’d go to the roof.

10
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:57.300
We had rented a gin pole from AAA, which is the thing you ratchet heavy things up with as you build a tower.

11
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.680
I had climbing gear because I was outdoorsy.

12
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:09.700
I made a couple of Swiss seats, which are the seats that you use when you’re climbing that you can hook on to.

13
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.000
I made a couple of them out of webbing . . . webbing, and rope, and crossing my fingers.

14
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.380
A couple of carabiners for the people who would actually be up on the tower.

15
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.440
Dave and Paul Burt climbed the tower and we were tensioning the wires,

16
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:32.000
and tensioning the guy lines, and putting in bolts, and getting the transmitter . . . I’m sorry,

17
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.680
getting the antenna up there and . . . We did this all when no one was looking,

18
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.460
and all the sudden the dean looks up and says, “How the hell did that get up there?”

19
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.060
About two years later [laughs].

20
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.460
Nobody noticed it.

21
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.900
But, you know, all the sudden, it’s like, “We got it!

22
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:47.660
We’re going to broadcast.

23
00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.380
We’re building that tower.”

24
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.660
And when the university said,

25
00:01:50.660 --> 00:02:00.400
“Well we have to put in an application and we have to put in for a . . . We have to put in for a work order and that might take six months,”

26
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:06.540
We all looked at each other and said, “We’re doing this.”

27
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.940
And that was entirely built by students.

28
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.620
Lo and behold, it’s still there.
